CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe autonomic hyperarousal, including marked resting tachycardia (HR 110), elevated blood pressure (145/92), and a 48-hour cessation of sleep, paradoxically triggered by a highly positive life event. Immediate out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was critical to perform a cardiovascular examination to definitively exclude an acute cardiac event or dangerous arrhythmia, and to provide immediate pharmacological down-regulation of the nervous system. Delaying care until routine hours would have prolonged the severe physical panic and insomnia, risking further physiological exhaustion, thereby justifying urgent medical intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered a single dose of oral diazepam 5mg to rapidly reduce sympathetic drive and force a break in the insomnia cycle. Advised the usual GP to arrange psychometric profiling demonstrating high resilience scores paired with evaluating elevated baseline resting cortisol.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the post-survival imposter syndrome safely, implement a "factual anchoring" technique: write down three objective, undeniable facts regarding why the promotion was earned (e.g., "I increased sales by 20%"); reading these facts aloud when panic rises relies on concrete data to override emotional fear. To address the inability to tolerate safety, utilize a "controlled chaos" physical outlet: engage in 20 minutes of high-intensity interval training daily; this provides the nervous system with the physical spike and release of adrenaline it craves, within a safe, controlled framework.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a trauma-informed executive coach or psychotherapist.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 72 hours for blood pressure reassessment and to discuss long-term psychological integration strategies.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or contact 000 if experiencing crushing central chest pain, shortness of breath at rest, or if the racing heart does not slow down after medication and rest.

